From 2fe9327769a8f40f1f1aad01c4feb35614ed6c18 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: srymsjackson <jstrazzo22@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 20:31:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add security headers middleware: CSP, HSTS, and related
 browser hardening

New app/security_headers.py, wired in globally via app.add_middleware() in
main.py. Every response now gets:

- Content-Security-Policy -- script-src restricted to 'self' plus a fresh
  per-request nonce (no 'unsafe-inline'), so an XSS gap the existing HTML
  escaping ever missed still can't run injected inline script or an
  attacker-hosted <script src>. style-src stays 'unsafe-inline' pragmatically
  (every HTML route uses inline <style> blocks; none interpolate
  caller-supplied text into one, and CSS injection has a much smaller blast
  radius than script injection). frame-ancestors 'none', object-src 'none',
  base-uri 'self', form-action 'self', connect-src 'self'.
- X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff, Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin,
  X-Frame-Options: DENY (belt-and-suspenders alongside frame-ancestors for
  older browsers), Permissions-Policy denying geolocation/microphone/camera
  (unused by this app; deny explicitly rather than leaving default-allow).
- Strict-Transport-Security, production only -- would break local dev over
  plain http otherwise.

GET /demo is the one route with an inline <script> block (a public,
unauthenticated page rendering caller-supplied text transcribed from live
calls), so it now takes the per-request nonce from request.state.csp_nonce
and embeds it in <script nonce="...">, keeping script-src's nonce
requirement meaningful instead of falling back to 'unsafe-inline' there too.

Tests: tests/test_security_headers.py (12 new tests) -- header presence
across routes, CSP directive correctness, nonce/header consistency, a fresh
nonce per request, HSTS present/absent by environment, and that
BaseHTTPMiddleware doesn't break the /ws/leads WebSocket route. Full suite:
90/90 passing. Verified test_demo_script_nonce_matches_csp_header actually
catches a regression by removing the nonce from the script tag and
re-running (fails as expected).
